<p>U.S.-brokered Russia-Ukraine talks in Geneva ended after two days with no public breakthrough. Both sides called the discussions “difficult,” and reports said the second day wrapped after about two hours. Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy accused Moscow of slowing the process, while Russia’s lead negotiator Vladimir Medinsky said the sessions were “business-like”. Negotiators focused on politically sensitive issues, including the status of occupied territories in eastern Ukraine and the fate of the Russian-controlled Zaporizhzhia nuclear power plant. Neither side signaled it had moved off its core demands. Still, Zelenskyy struck a slightly brighter note: he said there was “progress” on the military track, even as the political track amounted to “dialogue,” a small sign of momentum amid the stalemate. Delegations said they would meet again but gave no date, with the war nearing its fourth anniversary next week.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/2J-eUN0QcnvUMLxs1ounH8ytLjynDkv7XOIhNHHyq1Os3qcxB3SNHb2d_hTtImWFZvAS0xiIBZMTFSYME2i3i1zz9cfaNbuofuzNjkaSm_cR' alt='Ukraine President Volodymyr Zelensky holds a meeting with the Ukrainian team before negotiations in Geneva on Feb 18.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Extra talks: After the formal session ended, Medinsky and Ukrainian representatives kept talking for nearly two more hours, according to a representative of Kyiv’s chief negotiator Rustem Umerov.</li><li>Battlefield context: One account said Ukraine has been recapturing territory at its fastest pace in years through localized counterattacks in the southeast, with analysts pointing to disruptions in Russian communications and command.</li><li>Political pressure backdrop: Coverage said U.S. President Donald Trump has pressed Ukraine to accept a deal that could require painful concessions, as Russian forces continue striking Ukraine’s power grid and advancing.</li></ul><blockquote>We can state that Russia is trying to drag out negotiations that could already have reached the final stage, - Volodymyr Zelenskyy</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Ukraine (Zelenskyy): Zelenskyy said Russia was “playing for time” and trying to drag out talks that “could already have reached the final stage.” He also drew a line between the tracks, describing “progress” on the military side but only “dialogue” on political issues. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/ukraine-russia-war-us-talks-geneva-zelenskyy-says-putin-playing-for-time/'>CBS News</a>, <a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1974268/difficult-ukraine-talks-end-with-no-breakthrough'>Dawn</a>, <a href='http://www.euronews.com/2026/02/18/ukraine-us-russia-talks-progress-on-military-track-and-only-dialogue-on-political-aspect'>Euronews</a>)</li><li>Russia (Medinsky): Russian-linked accounts called the negotiations “very tense.” Medinsky—an ex-culture minister—has also drawn criticism from Ukrainian officials, who say he delivers “history lectures” instead of focusing on substantive bargaining. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1974268/difficult-ukraine-talks-end-with-no-breakthrough'>Dawn</a>, <a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/feb/18/ukraine-russia-peace-talks-zelenskyy-trump-putin-denmark-greenland-europe-live'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>United States (official stance): U.S. messaging varied across coverage, with one report saying Washington hailed “meaningful progress” even as Ukraine voiced disappointment. (<a href='https://www.firstpost.com/world/ukraine-dissatisfied-after-geneva-talks-but-us-hails-meaningful-progress-13981427.html'>Firstpost</a>)</li><li>US senators in Ukraine: A group of U.S. senators visiting Ukraine urged pressure on Russia alongside diplomacy. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/us-senators-in-ukraine-call-for-pressure-on-russia-not-just-talks'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2026/feb/18/ukraine-russia-peace-talks-zelenskyy-trump-pu